---
title: "Calculated Columns - API, Events & State"
description: "Manage $framework Data Grid calculated columns in code: read and update their column definitions, save and restore user-created columns with Grid State, and listen for calculated column events."
enterprise: true
---

[Calculated Columns](./calculated-columns/) are managed through column definitions, saved with [Grid State](./grid-state/), and reported through grid events.

## Managing Columns with Column Definitions

Manage calculated columns through column definitions, like other columns. Read the current definitions with `api.getColumnDefs()` and write them back with `api.setGridOption('columnDefs', ...)` to add, edit or remove a calculated column. To find the calculated columns in the grid, filter `api.getColumns()` by `calculatedExpression`.

When [processColDef](./calculated-columns-dialog/#processcoldef) is set, the definitions from `api.getColumnDefs()` include the callback's output. Loading them back as `columnDefs` declares those columns with that output fixed: the callback no longer runs for them, and the dialog shows the callback's `headerName` as the title.

The following example adds, edits and removes a Profit Margin column through these APIs, and logs calculated-column changes:

{% gridExampleRunner title="Calculated Columns API" name="calculated-columns-api" /%}

In the example, use the buttons to add, edit and remove Profit Margin, and click **Log Calculated Columns** to list the calculated columns in the grid.

## Saving and Restoring

The dialog manages calculated columns inside the grid without mutating the `columnDefs` array supplied by the application. To persist user-created calculated columns, read the complete current definitions from `api.getColumnDefs()`, or use [Grid State](./grid-state/), which captures them and recreates them on restore.

|                                 | Grid State                                                                                  | `api.getColumnDefs()`                                                                                                                     |
| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| User-created calculated columns | Captures them and recreates them on restore                                                 | Contains them in the complete current definitions                                                                                         |
| With `processColDef` set        | Keeps user-created columns with their own values, and applies the callback again on restore | Contains the callback's output. Loaded back as `columnDefs`, the columns keep that output and the callback no longer runs for them        |
| Dialog validation errors        | Preserves them                                                                              | Contains the column configuration, not validation errors. Restoring definitions alone treats the expressions as programmatic definitions. |

With `processColDef` set, use Grid State instead to keep user-created columns with their own values. Use Grid State to preserve dialog validation errors as well.

Grid State also captures user edits to, and removals of, calculated columns declared in `columnDefs`. On restore, it re-applies them over the declared definitions, so a stored state takes precedence over a changed declaration.

## Events

Use `calculatedColumnChanged` to track changes to `headerName`, `cellDataType`, `calculatedExpression` and `columnGroupShow`:

- The `changes` object contains only changed properties, with their previous and new values. Cell values read in the handler already reflect the change, including in calculated columns that depend on it.
- Deferred Apply raises one event for all changes. Live mode raises one per applied update, with [applyDebounce](./calculated-columns-dialog/#applydebounce) combining edits after a pause in typing. Initialisation and Grid State restoration do not raise this event.
- Check `expressionValidation.valid` before saving an accepted expression. When invalid, `expressionValidation.reason` is `restrictedReference`, `customValidation`, `unknownReference` or `invalidExpression`. Updates that change only the validation result also raise this event, with an empty `changes` object and the unchanged `expression`.

`calculatedColumnValidationStateChanged` reports changes to built-in validation status after an edit is applied, not while it is pending. Callback rejections are reported through `calculatedColumnChanged.expressionValidation` instead.

```js
const gridOptions = {
    onCalculatedColumnChanged: event => {
        const { headerName, cellDataType, calculatedExpression } = event.changes;
        if (headerName) {
            console.log('Title:', headerName.oldValue, headerName.newValue);
        }
        if (cellDataType) {
            console.log('Type:', cellDataType.oldValue, cellDataType.newValue);
        }
        if (calculatedExpression && event.expressionValidation.valid) {
            console.log('Expression:', calculatedExpression.oldValue, calculatedExpression.newValue);
        }
    },
};
```

{% note %}
Live expressions can be incomplete or invalid. Recognised references use stored `colId` values, such as `[revenue]`; references the dialog cannot resolve remain as typed, so they can still contain header names.

`expressionValidation` is a snapshot of syntax, direct reference resolution and saved dialog errors, not row-specific evaluation results. To persist rejected expressions with their blocked status, save [Grid State](./grid-state/); saving only the expression text does not retain that status.
{% /note %}

